# Threshold ideas for the "Evaluate thresholds" node

The code node is yours: every `if` you add is a monitoring rule. Patterns that work well:

## Live website
If you're monitoring a regular page (not an API), in the "Check service" node add, under Options → Response → "Include Response Headers and Status", and check:
```js
const code = Number($json.statusCode || 0);
if (code !== 200) {
  problems.push('The site responded with code ' + code);
}
```

## Growing queue
```js
const queued = Number(data.queued || 0);
if (queued > 50) {
  problems.push('Job queue at ' + queued + ' (threshold: 50)');
}
```

## Stale data (the system stopped updating)
```js
const last = new Date(data.last_updated || 0).getTime();
const hours = (Date.now() - last) / 3600000;
if (hours > 2) {
  problems.push('No updates for ' + hours.toFixed(1) + ' hours');
}
```

## Tips
- Start with ONE threshold and tighten it once it's gone a week without false alarms.
- Every alert should state the value and the threshold: "there are 34 (threshold: 20)" lets you decide from your phone.
- If a threshold fires every day and nobody acts, either the threshold is wrong or the action is missing: fix one of the two.
